CTF Field Guide — Part 4: Web Enumeration
Replace TARGET with the IP address of the TryHackMe machine.
1. Confirm the web service
nmap -p 80,443,8080,8000,8443 -sV TARGET
If a web port is open, test it in your browser:
http://TARGET
For a non-standard port:
http://TARGET:8080
Check the response from Terminal:
curl -I http://TARGET
Important headers include:
Server
X-Powered-By
Set-Cookie
Location
Content-Security-Policy
2. Identify technologies
whatweb http://TARGET
WhatWeb may identify:
- Apache or Nginx
- PHP
- WordPress
- JavaScript frameworks
- CMS software
- Cookies and redirects
You can also use Nmap:
nmap -p 80,443 --script http-title,http-headers TARGET
3. Inspect the page source
curl -s http://TARGET
Look for:
- HTML comments
- Hidden forms
- JavaScript files
- API paths
- Framework names
- Backup-file names
- Developer notes
Save the page:
curl -s http://TARGET -o index.html
Search it:
grep -inE "admin|login|api|backup|token|password|debug|secret" index.html
4. Check standard files
curl -i http://TARGET/robots.txt
curl -i http://TARGET/sitemap.xml
curl -i http://TARGET/.well-known/security.txt
These files sometimes reveal paths or security contacts.
5. Directory discovery with Gobuster
gobuster dir \
-u http://TARGET \
-w /usr/share/wordlists/dirb/common.txt \
-o gobuster.txt
Check common extensions:
gobuster dir \
-u http://TARGET \
-w /usr/share/wordlists/dirb/common.txt \
-x php,html,txt,bak,zip \
-o gobuster-extensions.txt
Useful status codes:
200 = page found
301/302 = redirect
403 = exists but access is forbidden
404 = not found
500 = server error
6. Directory discovery with FFUF
ffuf \
-u http://TARGET/FUZZ \
-w /usr/share/wordlists/dirb/common.txt \
-fc 404
Search for files:
ffuf \
-u http://TARGET/FUZZ \
-w /usr/share/wordlists/dirb/common.txt \
-e .php,.html,.txt,.bak \
-fc 404
- FUZZ is replaced by each word.
- -fc 404 hides normal not-found responses.
- -e adds file extensions.
7. Directory discovery with Feroxbuster
feroxbuster -u http://TARGET
With extensions:
feroxbuster -u http://TARGET -x php,html,txt,bak
Save results:
feroxbuster -u http://TARGET -o ferox.txt
Use one directory tool first. Running all of them immediately creates unnecessary noise.
8. Check virtual hosts
Sometimes several websites share one IP address.
First inspect the hostname:
curl -I http://TARGET
If the room gives you a domain such as example.thm, add it locally:
sudo nano /etc/hosts
Add:
TARGET example.thm
Then open:
http://example.thm
For authorised lab virtual-host discovery:
ffuf \
-u http://TARGET \
-H "Host: FUZZ.example.thm" \
-w /usr/share/seclists/Discovery/DNS/subdomains-top1million-5000.txt \
-fs 0
The Host header tells the server which virtual website you want.
9. Inspect JavaScript files
Find JavaScript references:
grep -oE 'src="[^"]+\.js[^"]*"' index.html
Download a discovered JavaScript file:
curl -s http://TARGET/app.js -o app.js
Search it:
grep -inE "api|admin|user|token|key|endpoint|debug" app.js
JavaScript may reveal:
- API routes
- Hidden page names
- Parameter names
- Development endpoints
- Front-end authentication logic
Do not treat a visible key as permission to access another system. It is only a lab clue.
10. Find parameters
If the site has a URL such as:
http://TARGET/page.php?id=1
record the parameter:
id
Inspect forms:
grep -inE "<form|input|action=|method=" index.html
Look for:
GET parameters
POST fields
File-upload forms
Search fields
Cookie names
For authorised parameter discovery with FFUF:
ffuf \
-u "http://TARGET/page.php?FUZZ=test" \
-w /usr/share/seclists/Discovery/Web-Content/burp-parameter-names.txt \
-fs 0
This is discovery only. Do not automatically assume a parameter is vulnerable.
11. Inspect cookies
curl -I http://TARGET
Look for:
Set-Cookie
HttpOnly
Secure
SameSite
A cookie missing security attributes may be worth documenting, but it is not automatically exploitable.
12. Test HTTP methods
curl -i -X OPTIONS http://TARGET
This may show supported methods such as:
GET
POST
HEAD
OPTIONS
PUT
DELETE
A method being listed does not prove it can be abused. Verify only within the authorised room.
13. Use Burp Suite
Start Burp:
burpsuite
Basic workflow:
1. Open Burp Suite.
2. Use the temporary project option.
3. Open Burp’s built-in browser.
4. Browse the target.
5. Inspect the HTTP history.
6. Send interesting requests to Repeater.
7. Change one value at a time.
8. Compare status codes, response length, and response content.
Burp is useful for understanding how the browser communicates with the web server.
14. Check for common web-server information
nikto -h http://TARGET
Nikto may identify:
- Dangerous default files
- Missing headers
- Exposed directories
- Old server software
- Backup files
It can produce false positives, so verify findings manually.
15. HTTPS and certificates
curl -k -I https://TARGET
View certificate information:
openssl s_client -connect TARGET:443
Look for:
- Hostnames
- Certificate names
- Expiry dates
- Alternative names
A certificate hostname may reveal another domain used by the application.
